<span class="kicker">2026-08-26 / RIDGEWELL GUIDES</span><h1>How to Finish Off the Sides of a Paver Walkway</h1>
</div>
</section>
<section class="section article-section"><div class="wrap editorial-layout"><article class="prose blog-content"><p>A paver walkway’s structural edge, the restraint holding pavers in place underground, isn’t the same thing as its visual edge, what you actually see when you look down at the finished walkway. That visual finish, the border treatment, the transition into surrounding lawn or beds, the plantings alongside it, is what separates a walkway that looks intentionally designed from one that looks like it just stops where the pavers ran out. Getting this right is often less about construction and more about a handful of design decisions that tie the hardscape into the rest of the yard.</p>
<p>At Ridgewell Landscape and Design, side and edge finishing is one of the details we spend real design time on for every walkway we build across the Denver area, including Broomfield, Parker, and the surrounding communities, since it has an outsized effect on how finished the whole project reads. This guide covers the main options for finishing the sides of a paver walkway, how they compare on cost and upkeep, how to handle the transition into different surrounding surfaces, and how to combine approaches for a cohesive, layered result.</p>
<h2><b>Common Ways to Finish the Sides of a Paver Walkway</b></h2>
<table><tbody><tr><td><b>Option</b></td><td><b>Look</b></td><td><b>Maintenance</b></td><td><b>Best For</b></td></tr><tr><td>Contrasting border course</td><td>Defined, framed edge</td><td>Low</td><td>Formal entries, defined pathways</td></tr><tr><td>Natural stone or boulder edging</td><td>Rustic, organic</td><td>Low to moderate</td><td>Mountain or naturalistic landscapes</td></tr><tr><td>Mulch bed border</td><td>Soft, garden-style</td><td>Moderate, needs refreshing</td><td>Garden paths, planting-heavy yards</td></tr><tr><td>Groundcover planting edge</td><td>Green, living border</td><td>Moderate</td><td>Softer, cottage-style landscapes</td></tr><tr><td>Gravel border</td><td>Clean, low-maintenance</td><td>Low</td><td>Modern or xeriscape-style yards</td></tr><tr><td>Direct lawn transition</td><td>Simple, blended</td><td>Moderate, edging upkeep</td><td>Traditional yards with turf</td></tr></tbody></table><p>None of these options are mutually exclusive, many finished walkways combine two or three, a contrasting border course right at the paver edge, followed by a planting bed a bit further out, for example.</p>
<h2><b>Why the Edge Treatment Matters as Much as the Walkway Itself</b></h2>
<p>It’s easy to spend most of the planning process focused on paver color, pattern, and material, and treat the edge as an afterthought once the main surface is done. In practice, the edge is where a walkway either blends convincingly into the rest of the landscape or looks like a strip of hardscape dropped into the yard without much thought. A well-chosen edge treatment does real work, it frames the walkway, manages the practical transition between materials, and often solves small maintenance headaches, like grass creeping onto pavers, before they start.</p>
<h2><b>Contrasting Border Course</b></h2>
<h3><b>What It Is</b></h3>
<p>A border course uses a different color, size, or material paver along the outer edge of the walkway, creating a defined frame around the main field. This is a purely decorative choice built into the paver installation itself, distinct from the structural edge restraint hidden underneath.</p>
<h3><b>Why It Works Well</b></h3>
<p>A contrasting border reads as intentional and finished, giving the walkway a clear, framed edge rather than an abrupt stop. It pairs particularly well with formal entries or walkways using a simple pattern like running bond, where the border adds visual interest without competing with an already busy pattern.</p>
<h3><b>Installation Considerations</b></h3>
<p>Because a border course is installed as part of the paver field itself, it needs to be planned before construction begins, not added afterward. Deciding on border width, typically one or two paver widths, and confirming it against your overall walkway dimensions early avoids awkward cuts or a border that reads as too thin or too heavy relative to the main field.</p>
<h2><b>Natural Stone or Boulder Edging</b></h2>
<h3><b>What It Is</b></h3>
<p>Larger natural stones or boulders placed along one or both sides of the walkway, either loosely arranged or more formally set, create a rugged, organic border that works especially well with a mountain or natural design aesthetic.</p>
<h3><b>Why It Works Well in Colorado Landscapes</b></h3>
<p>This option pairs naturally with the kind of mountain-inspired landscaping common across the Denver area, echoing the natural stone found throughout the region’s terrain. It also requires very little ongoing maintenance once placed, since the stones themselves don’t need refreshing the way mulch or plantings do.</p>
<h3><b>Sourcing and Placement</b></h3>
<p>Boulder and larger stone edging works best when placed with some intentional irregularity rather than in a perfectly even row, which reads as more artificial than natural. Mixing a few different stone sizes along the edge, rather than uniform pieces, generally produces a more convincing, natural-looking result.</p>
<h2><b>Mulch Bed Border</b></h2>
<h3><b>What It Is</b></h3>
<p>A planting bed filled with mulch running alongside the walkway, either bare or interplanted with shrubs, perennials, or seasonal color, creating a soft, garden-style transition from the hardscape into the rest of the yard.</p>
<h3><b>Maintenance Considerations</b></h3>
<p>Mulch beds need periodic refreshing, typically once a year, to maintain their appearance and continue suppressing weeds effectively. This is a bit more ongoing upkeep than a stone or gravel border, but it offers more flexibility for seasonal plantings and color changes.</p>
<h3><b>Choosing Mulch Type</b></h3>
<p>Bark mulch is the most common choice and breaks down gradually, enriching the soil as it does. Rock or gravel mulch alternatives last longer without needing annual refreshing but don’t offer the same soil benefits. For a walkway border specifically, a mid-weight bark mulch that resists washing out during heavy rain is generally the more practical choice over lighter, finer mulch products.</p>
<h2><b>Groundcover Planting Edge</b></h2>
<h3><b>What It Is</b></h3>
<p>Low, spreading plants, like creeping thyme, sedum, or ornamental grasses, planted directly alongside the walkway edge create a green, living border rather than a hardscape or mulch treatment.</p>
<h3><b>Why It Works Well</b></h3>
<p>Groundcover softens the hardscape’s edge more than any of the other options here, and many varieties can handle occasional light foot traffic spilling slightly over the walkway’s edge without damage. This option does require some establishment time and ongoing care compared to a stone or gravel border.</p>
<h3><b>Choosing the Right Groundcover</b></h3>
<p>Drought-tolerant, low-growing varieties tend to perform best alongside a walkway in this climate, since they establish well without heavy irrigation and don’t require frequent trimming to stay low and tidy. Creeping thyme in particular has become a popular choice for exactly this application, since it tolerates occasional foot traffic and releases a pleasant scent when brushed against.</p>

